{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently forecasts smooth, monotonic trends (either flat or slow drift) while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude spikes and sharp reversals. Apply a volatility multiplier to the forecast range, particularly when recent history shows price jumps exceeding 5%.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after spikes: When true prices spike (e.g., from 80 to 88), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent drop. If a price spike occurs, immediately reduce the forecast for the following 5-10 days by 3-5 EUR to account for the inevitable pullback.",
    "Adjust for sentiment lag in downtrends: In periods of declining prices (e.g., Nov 2023), the model maintains a flat or slightly upward bias despite negative or neutral sentiment. If sentiment is neutral/negative and prices are falling, force a downward slope on the forecast rather than a flat line.",
    "Reduce forecast horizon bias: The model systematically overestimates prices in the mid-to-long term (days 15-30) during volatile regimes. Apply a decay factor to the forecast values as the horizon extends beyond 10 days to prevent the 'flatline' overestimation.",
    "Trigger regime shift detection: If the 3-day moving average of true prices diverges significantly from the model's flat forecast, switch from a trend-following logic to a mean-reversion logic immediately, as the model fails to adapt to structural breaks."
  ]
}
